    "result_text": "All test classes are acceptance tests, and all ran green. The implementation is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the rule that rejects creating an owner when the owner's city already contains 50 or more owners, returning **409 Conflict**. The change follows the existing exception-driven pattern used by the other create-time business rules (duplicate telephone, duplicate household).\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **New exception** \u2014 `rest/controller/CityOwnerLimitExceededException.java`: a `RuntimeException` carrying the conflicting city and the limit, mirroring `DuplicateTelephoneException`/`DuplicateHouseholdException`.\n\n2. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 added a `rejectCityAtCapacity(owner.getCity())` call (placed alongside the other rejection checks) plus a private helper with a `CITY_OWNER_LIMIT = 50` constant. It counts existing owners whose city matches case-insensitively (consistent with the per-city customer-code sequencing) and throws when the count is \u2265 50. The count is taken before the new owner is persisted, so it excludes the one being created.\n\n3. **`ExceptionControllerAdvice`** \u2014 added an `@ExceptionHandler` for `CityOwnerLimitExceededException` mapping it to `HttpStatus.CONFLICT` (409) with a `ProblemDetail` body and `errors: [\"city\"]`, matching the other 409 handlers.\n\nThe new acceptance test `Cp18Tests.coreRejectsWhenCityAtCapacity` (fill a city to exactly 50, then expect the 51st to 409) passes, and the full suite is green: **29 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS**.",
